WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.060
SECOND NIGHT Well, you've made it through, she

2
00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:06.800
said, holding both my hands tightly.

3
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.040
I've been here for the past two hours.

4
00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.100
You have no idea what a state I've

5
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:11.900
been in all day.

6
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:13.900
I know, I know.

7
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:15.920
But let's get down to business.

8
00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:17.580
Do you know why I came?

9
00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.960
Not to talk nonsense like yesterday.

10
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:21.420
Here's the thing.

11
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.820
We need to act more sensibly from now

12
00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:24.120
on.

13
00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:26.420
I thought a lot about this last night.

14
00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:28.320
In what way?

15
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.259
What do you mean by more sensibly?

16
00:00:31.759 --> 00:00:33.880
I'm ready to do my part, but honestly,

17
00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.660
nothing has ever seemed more sensible to me

18
00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:37.480
than this.

19
00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:39.340
What's happening right now?

20
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:40.900
Really?

21
00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:43.560
Well, first of all, please don't squeeze my

22
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:44.460
hand so hard.

23
00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.000
Secondly, I have to tell you, I spent

24
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.780
a lot of time thinking about you today,

25
00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.580
and I wasn't sure what to make of

26
00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:50.760
it.

27
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:52.020
And?

28
00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:53.520
How did it end?

29
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.840
How did it end?

30
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.740
Well, I realized that we have to start

31
00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:02.000
fresh, because after thinking it over, I concluded

32
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.400
that I don't know you at all.

33
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.540
Last night, I acted like a child, like

34
00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:07.440
a silly little girl.

35
00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.180
I've decided it's my soft heart that's to

36
00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:10.480
blame.

37
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.840
That's what we always do, isn't it?

38
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.920
We analyze our actions and end up excusing

39
00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:16.400
ourselves.

40
00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.400
But to fix my mistake, I've decided I

41
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.060
need to learn everything about you.

42
00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.880
Since there's no one I can ask, you'll

43
00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:24.920
have to tell me yourself.

44
00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:26.860
So what kind of person are you?

45
00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:28.700
Come on, start from the beginning.

46
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.140
Tell me your whole story.

47
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.080
My story?

48
00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:33.440
I repeated nervously.

49
00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:34.860
My story?

50
00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.500
But who said I have a story?

51
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:38.840
I don't have one.

52
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.020
Then how have you lived if you don't

53
00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:42.680
have a story?

54
00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:44.080
She asked, laughing.

55
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.080
I've lived without any story at all.

56
00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:52.740
I've lived keeping to myself, completely alone, utterly

57
00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:53.320
alone.

58
00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.540
Do you know what it means to be

59
00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:55.900
alone?

60
00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:57.660
Alone?

61
00:01:58.620 --> 00:01:59.840
What do you mean?

62
00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:01.680
Do you never see anyone?

63
00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:04.560
Oh, I see people, of course.

64
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:06.440
But even so, I'm alone.

65
00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:07.660
What?

66
00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:09.440
Do you mean you never talk to anyone?

67
00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:13.060
Not really, not in any meaningful way.

68
00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:15.220
Then who are you?

69
00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:16.380
Explain yourself.

70
00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:18.240
Wait, I think I know.

71
00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:19.880
You must be like me.

72
00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:21.780
Do you have a grandmother?

73
00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.620
Mine is blind, and she never lets me

74
00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:25.300
go anywhere.

75
00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.060
I've almost forgotten how to talk to people.

76
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:30.780
Two years ago, when I got into trouble

77
00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.860
and couldn't be controlled, she made me sit

78
00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:33.240
with her.

79
00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.560
She even pinned my dress to hers, and

80
00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:37.080
now we sit like that every day.

81
00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.640
She knits stockings, even though she's blind, and

82
00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:42.820
I sit next to her, sewing or reading

83
00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:43.220
aloud.

84
00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:45.840
For two years now, we've been stuck like

85
00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:46.140
that.

86
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:47.780
It's such a strange habit.

87
00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.220
My God, how awful!

88
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.580
No, I don't have a grandmother like that.

89
00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:56.020
Well, then, if you don't, why do you

90
00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:57.200
stay home all the time?

91
00:02:58.040 --> 00:02:59.800
Do you really want to know what kind

92
00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:00.740
of person I am?

93
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:02.660
Yes, I do.

94
00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:04.800
In the strict sense of the word?

95
00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.880
In the very strictest sense of the word.

96
00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:10.560
Very well, I am a type.

97
00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:12.160
A type?

98
00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:12.940
A type?

99
00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:14.660
What sort of type?

100
00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.460
the girl exclaimed, laughing as though she hadn't

101
00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:18.920
had the chance to laugh in a whole

102
00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:19.260
year.

103
00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.360
Yes, it's so amusing talking to you.

104
00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:23.620
Look, here's a bench.

105
00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:24.780
Let's sit down.

106
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.540
No one will walk by here.

107
00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.200
No one will overhear us.

108
00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:29.780
Now tell me your story.

109
00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.460
Don't try to deny it.

110
00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:32.900
I know you have a story.

111
00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.040
You're just hiding it.

112
00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:36.520
To start with, what is a type?

113
00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:38.300
A type?

114
00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.260
A type is someone original.

115
00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:43.800
It's a ridiculous person, I replied, catching her

116
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:45.500
contagious, childlike laughter.

117
00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:46.660
It's a character.

118
00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.540
Listen, do you know what it means to

119
00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:50.120
be a dreamer?

120
00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:51.840
A dreamer?

121
00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:52.980
Of course I do.

122
00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:54.440
I'm a dreamer myself.

123
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.680
Sometimes when I sit with my grandmother, all

124
00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.200
sorts of things fill my head.

125
00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:02.860
Why, when I start dreaming, my imagination runs

126
00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:03.340
wild.

127
00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:05.720
I've even married a Chinese prince in my

128
00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:06.200
dreams.

129
00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:08.640
But sometimes dreaming is a good thing.

130
00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:11.120
Though goodness knows it's better when you have

131
00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:13.880
something real to think about instead, she added,

132
00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:15.680
her tone suddenly more serious.

133
00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:16.700
Wonderful.

134
00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:19.440
If you've been married to a Chinese emperor,

135
00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:21.560
then you'll understand me perfectly.

136
00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:23.240
Come, listen.

137
00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:25.320
Oh, but wait a moment.

138
00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.760
I don't even know your name yet.

139
00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:29.460
Finally!

140
00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:32.520
You sure took your time thinking of that.

141
00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:36.080
My goodness, it never even occurred to me

142
00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:36.460
before.

143
00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:38.780
I felt perfectly happy without knowing.

144
00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:41.180
My name is Nastenka.

145
00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:42.880
Nastenka.

146
00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:44.140
And that's it?

147
00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:45.240
Nothing more?

148
00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:47.600
Nothing more?

149
00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.220
Why, isn't that enough for you, you insatiable

150
00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:51.680
person?

151
00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:53.680
Not enough?

152
00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.540
On the contrary, it's more than enough.

153
00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:58.240
A great deal, actually.

154
00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:00.900
Nastenka, you kind girl.

155
00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.200
If you're Nastenka to me from the start,

156
00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:04.360
that's all I need.

157
00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:06.520
Exactly, well.

158
00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:08.500
Well, listen, Nastenka.

159
00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:10.560
Here's my ridiculous story.

160
00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:14.260
I sat beside her, put on a mockingly

161
00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:17.700
serious expression, and began speaking as though reading

162
00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:18.380
from a book.

163
00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:22.420
There are, Nastenka, though you might not realize

164
00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:25.060
it, strange little corners in Petersburg.

165
00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:28.320
It's as if the same sun that shines

166
00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.840
on the rest of Petersburg doesn't reach these

167
00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:31.240
spots.

168
00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.820
Instead, they seem to have a different sun,

169
00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:37.900
one made just for them, casting a unique

170
00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:38.900
light on everything.

171
00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:43.220
In these hidden corners, my dear Nastenka, life

172
00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:45.540
is very different from the bustling world around

173
00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:45.820
us.

174
00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:47.700
It's a life that seems to belong to

175
00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.740
some far-off, unknown place, not to our

176
00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:52.080
overly serious times.

177
00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:54.560
This life is a mix of the wildly

178
00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:59.940
imaginative and passionately idealistic, combined, unfortunately, with

179
00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:03.500
something drab and dull, plainly ordinary, if not

180
00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:04.420
downright vulgar.

181
00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:07.980
Wow, good heavens, what an introduction!

182
00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:09.460
What am I hearing?

183
00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:11.400
Listen, Nastenka.

184
00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:13.800
I feel like I'll never get tired of

185
00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:14.900
calling you Nastenka.

186
00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:17.900
Let me tell you that in these little

187
00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:20.740
corners strange people live, dreamers.

188
00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:23.580
A dreamer, if you want an exact definition,

189
00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:25.360
isn't really a regular person.

190
00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:27.080
There's something in between.

191
00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:30.600
Most of the time they settle into some

192
00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.520
hidden, unreachable corner, as if they're trying to

193
00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:34.460
escape the daylight.

194
00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:37.760
Once they find their little spot, they stick

195
00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:39.740
to it like a snail, or maybe more

196
00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:42.360
like a tortoise, that odd creature which is

197
00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.240
both an animal and its own house.

198
00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:46.960
Now why do you think they're so attached

199
00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:49.700
to their four walls, which are always painted

200
00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:54.400
some dull green, dirty, gloomy, and always smelling

201
00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:55.960
horribly of tobacco smoke?

202
00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:59.540
And why, when one of their rare acquaintances

203
00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:02.280
visits them, because eventually they manage to drive

204
00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:05.240
away all their friends, why does this strange

205
00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:07.680
person greet them with such awkwardness?

206
00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:11.820
They seem embarrassed, their face changes, and they

207
00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:14.120
look like they've just committed some crime within

208
00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:16.920
those four walls, like they've been forging fake

209
00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:19.200
money or writing poems to send to a

210
00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:22.200
magazine, pretending the real poet has passed away,

211
00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:24.420
and they feel obligated to get the work

212
00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:24.900
published.

213
00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:28.680
Why tell me, Nastenka, is it so hard

214
00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:30.740
for these two to have a normal conversation?

215
00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:32.860
Why is there no laughter?

216
00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:36.320
Why does the visitor, who might otherwise love

217
00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:38.660
to joke around and chat about light-hearted

218
00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:42.280
things, like women or other fun topics, suddenly

219
00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:43.020
fall silent?

220
00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:46.220
And why does this visitor, who is probably

221
00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:49.220
new and making their first visit—because, trust me,

222
00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:52.300
there won't be a second—why does even this

223
00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:55.600
friend become so uncomfortable and tongue-tied, even

224
00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:56.660
if they're normally witty?

225
00:07:57.660 --> 00:07:59.600
It's as if they're trapped by the gloomy

226
00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:01.280
expression on their host's face.

227
00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:04.720
And why does the host, in turn, seem

228
00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:08.620
completely lost, desperately trying to liven things up,

229
00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:11.620
show some charm, and talk about cheerful things

230
00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:13.480
like women to make the visit enjoyable?

231
00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:17.080
And why does this visitor, suddenly remembering some

232
00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:20.140
urgent task that doesn't really exist, grab their

233
00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:21.780
hat and hurry to leave?

234
00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:25.040
They pull their hand away from their host's

235
00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:27.980
overly warm handshake, as the host tries their

236
00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:30.660
best to express regret and salvage the failed

237
00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:31.039
meeting.

238
00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:34.120
Why does the visitor chuckle on their way

239
00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:36.940
out, promising themselves never to visit this odd

240
00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:37.720
person again?

241
00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:40.760
And yet, at the same time, they can't

242
00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:43.000
help but imagine that the host's face, during

243
00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:46.260
their conversation, looked exactly like a poor kitten,

244
00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:50.480
caught by some mischievous children, roughly handled, scared,

245
00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:54.720
and teased until, utterly humiliated, it hides under

246
00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:55.840
a chair in the dark.

247
00:08:56.620 --> 00:09:00.060
There it bristles up, hisses, and washes its

248
00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:02.720
face with its little paws, sulking over its

249
00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:05.520
ordeal and glaring at the world, even at

250
00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:07.860
the scraps of food the kind housekeeper left

251
00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:08.320
for it.

252
00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:12.680
Wait, Nastenka interrupted, her eyes wide and her

253
00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:14.820
little mouth open in surprise as she listened

254
00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:15.180
to me.

255
00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:16.320
Wait.

256
00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:19.440
I don't know why all that happened, or

257
00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:21.680
why you're asking me such ridiculous questions.

258
00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:24.280
All I know is, this must have happened

259
00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:25.700
to you exactly like that.

260
00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:29.840
Doubtless, I replied with the most serious expression.

261
00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:33.020
Well, since there's no doubt about it, go

262
00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:36.320
on, said Nastenka, because I really want to

263
00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:37.900
know how it all ends.

264
00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:42.520
You want to know, Nastenka, what our hero,

265
00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:45.140
that is, me, since I was the hero

266
00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:47.820
of the whole affair, was doing in his

267
00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:48.440
little corner?

268
00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:51.340
You want to know why the unexpected visit

269
00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:53.900
of a friend left me so flustered, why

270
00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:55.680
I felt upset the entire day?

271
00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:57.860
You want to know why I completely lost

272
00:09:57.860 --> 00:10:00.420
my composure, why I blushed when the door

273
00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:03.020
opened, why I was so incapable of hosting

274
00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:05.420
my visitor, and why I crumbled under the

275
00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:06.900
weight of my own hospitality?

276
00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:10.500
