538
00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:21.400
And then you realize, Nastenka, that you've outgrown

539
00:21:21.400 --> 00:21:24.460
your dreams, that the ideals you once cherished

540
00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:26.660
are crumbling, shattering into dust.

541
00:21:27.500 --> 00:21:29.400
And yet what else can you do but

542
00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:31.460
try to build something new from the broken

543
00:21:31.460 --> 00:21:31.960
pieces?

544
00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:35.600
All the while your soul craves something else,

545
00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:38.520
something real, something it cannot name.

546
00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:42.440
And yet in vain the dreamer searches through

547
00:21:42.440 --> 00:21:45.080
his old dreams, hoping to find a spark

548
00:21:45.080 --> 00:21:48.600
among the ashes, just one ember to reignite

549
00:21:48.600 --> 00:21:51.300
the fire, to warm his cold heart once

550
00:21:51.300 --> 00:21:51.640
more.

551
00:21:52.420 --> 00:21:55.620
He tries to revive that sweet, intoxicating feeling

552
00:21:55.620 --> 00:21:57.900
that once made his blood race and his

553
00:21:57.900 --> 00:22:00.820
eyes fill with tears, that once deceived him

554
00:22:00.820 --> 00:22:01.660
so beautifully.

555
00:22:02.440 --> 00:22:03.760
But it's all for nothing.

556
00:22:04.600 --> 00:22:07.240
Do you understand, Nastenka, what I've come to?

557
00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:10.320
Do you know what I do now, out

558
00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:11.260
of sheer desperation?

559
00:22:12.220 --> 00:22:15.740
I celebrate the anniversaries of my feelings, yes,

560
00:22:15.900 --> 00:22:18.340
anniversaries of things that never truly happened.

561
00:22:19.040 --> 00:22:21.600
I mark the dates of those foolish, shadowy

562
00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:24.060
dreams that I once believed in, dreams that

563
00:22:24.060 --> 00:22:26.560
no longer exist because I've lost the power

564
00:22:26.560 --> 00:22:27.340
to create them.

565
00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:30.260
Even dreams, you see, require effort.

566
00:22:30.740 --> 00:22:31.780
They don't come for free.

567
00:22:32.600 --> 00:22:35.060
And now I find myself revisiting the places

568
00:22:35.060 --> 00:22:37.020
where I once felt happy in my own

569
00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:37.360
way.

570
00:22:38.080 --> 00:22:40.040
I try to piece together my present life

571
00:22:40.040 --> 00:22:42.120
from the fragments of an unchangeable past.

572
00:22:42.940 --> 00:22:45.180
Like a ghost, I wander the streets and

573
00:22:45.180 --> 00:22:48.780
alleys of Petersburg, aimless and sad, searching for

574
00:22:48.780 --> 00:22:49.220
memories.

575
00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:53.060
Do you know what it's like, Nastenka, to

576
00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:55.400
stand on a familiar street and remember that

577
00:22:55.400 --> 00:22:57.580
a year ago to the day you were

578
00:22:57.580 --> 00:23:00.700
there, just as lonely, just as lost?

579
00:23:01.420 --> 00:23:04.680
And yet, somehow, it feels as though things

580
00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:07.180
were better then, even though they weren't.

581
00:23:08.120 --> 00:23:10.360
Back then, your dreams might have been sad,

582
00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:13.160
but they weren't haunted by the black thoughts

583
00:23:13.160 --> 00:23:14.400
that torment you now.

584
00:23:14.920 --> 00:23:17.860
You didn't feel the relentless gnawing of conscience,

585
00:23:18.380 --> 00:23:21.420
that gloomy, unyielding guilt that eats away at

586
00:23:21.420 --> 00:23:22.260
you day and night.

587
00:23:22.720 --> 00:23:25.140
And you ask yourself, where have all my

588
00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:25.920
dreams gone?

589
00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:28.200
You shake your head and think about how

590
00:23:28.200 --> 00:23:29.740
quickly the years have flown by.

591
00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:31.880
And then comes the awful question.

592
00:23:32.540 --> 00:23:34.060
What have you done with your time?

593
00:23:34.660 --> 00:23:36.560
Where have you buried your best days?

594
00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:38.720
Have you even truly lived?

595
00:23:39.520 --> 00:23:42.740
And so you look around and see how

596
00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:44.340
cold the world has become.

597
00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:46.780
You think about how, in just a few

598
00:23:46.780 --> 00:23:49.720
more years, solitude will close in completely.

599
00:23:50.600 --> 00:23:53.720
Then will come old age, creeping in with

600
00:23:53.720 --> 00:23:57.240
its trembling hands, and after that misery, desolation.

601
00:23:58.160 --> 00:24:00.280
Your imagined world will fade.

602
00:24:00.940 --> 00:24:03.560
Your dreams will die and fall like autumn

603
00:24:03.560 --> 00:24:06.700
leaves from the trees, leaving you utterly alone.

604
00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:11.020
Oh, Nastenka, can you imagine what it will

605
00:24:11.020 --> 00:24:13.480
feel like to reach that point, to look

606
00:24:13.480 --> 00:24:16.460
back and find you have nothing, absolutely nothing,

607
00:24:16.820 --> 00:24:19.180
to regret because everything you've lost was never

608
00:24:19.180 --> 00:24:20.420
real in the first place?

609
00:24:21.020 --> 00:24:23.640
Can you imagine realizing that all you've ever

610
00:24:23.640 --> 00:24:27.320
had were dreams, foolish, empty dreams?

611
00:24:28.580 --> 00:24:31.260
Come now, don't stir up my feelings anymore,

612
00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:34.400
said Nastenka, brushing away a tear that had

613
00:24:34.400 --> 00:24:35.420
slipped down her cheek.

614
00:24:35.880 --> 00:24:37.920
It's enough, it's all over now.

615
00:24:38.340 --> 00:24:40.720
From this moment we're together, two of us.

616
00:24:41.580 --> 00:24:44.140
Whatever happens to me, we won't be separated.

617
00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:45.460
